explanations.E0018:
# E0018: `mut self` on a method

A method of a class or an enum takes `self`, never `mut self`, and a method an interface requires cannot take `mut self` either. A method of an `extend` block follows the rule of the class, the enum or the struct that the block extends. A method of a struct cannot take `mut self` yet: take `self` and return the changed copy.

## Why

An object is shared, so `self` can already write every `mut` field of the class. A `mut self` would suggest that the method could replace the object, which no method can do. An enum value never changes, so `mut self` on an enum method has nothing to mean; the help is to return a new value. Removing the `mut` is an unsafe fix there, since the body may still assign `self`.

An interface method has no body, so it cannot change anything. A class that conforms to the interface changes its object's `mut` fields through plain `self`, so the requirement is `fn bump(self)`. Removing the `mut` is a safe fix when `self` is the whole parameter, as in a class.

When `self` has a type, as in `mut self: i64`, the fix also removes the type, because `self` takes none.

## Broken

```sisu
class Counter {
    mut n: i64

    fn bump(mut self) {
        self.n += 1
    }
}

fn main() {
    let c = Counter(n: 0)
    c.bump()
    print(c.n)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Counter {
    mut n: i64

    fn bump(self) {
        self.n += 1
    }
}

fn main() {
    let c = Counter(n: 0)
    c.bump()
    print(c.n)
}
```

explanations.E0118:
# E0118: unknown variant

`Enum.Name` builds the variant `Name` of the enum, so the enum must declare a variant of that name.

## Why

An enum lists every variant it has; there are no others to build. When a variant's name is close to the one written, the error suggests it, but as an unsafe fix: the closest name is not always the one you meant.

## Broken

```sisu
enum Expr {
    Num(i64)
}

fn main() {
    let _ = Expr.Nmu(2)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
enum Expr {
    Num(i64)
}

fn main() {
    let _ = Expr.Num(2)
}
```

explanations.E0120:
# E0120: variant or method used as the other

The methods of a struct or an enum are called on a value, `e.eval()`. The variants of an enum are built through the enum, `Expr.Num(1)`. A method named through its type, or a variant called on a value, is an error.

## Why

Sisu has one way to build a variant and one way to call a method. So `Expr.eval` never reads as a function value, and `e.Num(1)` never reads as a method. The error names the form that works.

A call such as `Expr.eval(e)` has a fix when its first argument has the type. The fix moves that argument before the `.`: `e.eval()`. It is safe when the method takes the other arguments. It is unsafe when one of them does not fit, or stands at a `mut` parameter and has no `mut`.

Any other form has no fix, since the value to call the method on is yours to choose. That is, the first argument has another type, is optional or has `mut`, or the call has a label, a type argument or no argument.

## Broken

```sisu
enum Expr {
    Num(i64)
}

fn make(e: Expr) -> Expr {
    e.Num(1)
}

fn main() {
    match make(Expr.Num(0)) {
        Num(n) => print(n)
    }
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
enum Expr {
    Num(i64)
}

fn make(e: Expr) -> Expr {
    match e {
        Num(n) => Expr.Num(n + 1)
    }
}

fn main() {
    match make(Expr.Num(0)) {
        Num(n) => print(n)
    }
}
```
